# LOTA EK manufacturer root sources -- template.
#
# This ships empty on purpose: a pin is only worth anything when you have
# verified it out of band against a value the vendor controls. Copy this to
# `sources`, then add one line per root your fleet's EK certificates chain
# to. Every line here is a comment, so an unedited copy produces no bundle.
#
# One line per trusted vendor root:
#
#   <sha256>  <filename>  <url>  <vendor label...>
#
#   sha256    lowercase hex SHA-256 over the certificate DER. Verify this
#             out of band against the vendor's published fingerprint before
#             trusting a root -- this pin is the whole point of the bundle.
#             Never trust the value the download itself hands you.
#   filename  bare name the root is written under in the bundle directory.
#   url       distribution point the root is fetched from.
#   label     free text, for the manifest and diagnostics.
#
# lota-ek-roots-update.sh fetches each url, recomputes the DER SHA-256, and
# refuses to write any root whose fingerprint does not match the pin.
#
# The supported hardware set is not a fixed vendor list -- it is every TPM
# whose EK root you can verify and pin. The reliable way to find the right
# root for a platform is to walk an actual EK certificate up to its
# self-signed root:
#
#   # discrete TPM / Intel PTT: EK cert lives in TPM NV
#   sudo tpm2_nvreadpublic                       # find the 0x01c0xxxx EK cert
#   sudo tpm2_nvread 0x01c00002 -o ek.der        # RSA EK (0x01c0000a = ECC)
#   openssl x509 -in ek.der -inform DER -noout -issuer -ext authorityInfoAccess
#   # Windows host: PowerShell (admin)
#   #   $ek = Get-TpmEndorsementKeyInfo -HashAlgorithm Sha256
#   #   $ek.ManufacturerCertificates + $ek.AdditionalCertificates
#   # follow each "CA Issuers" URL up until issuer == subject (the root),
#   # then: sha256sum root.der  -> the pin (after out-of-band confirmation).
#
# See configs/ek-roots/README.rst for the full discovery and verification flow.
#
# Two manufacturers publish a root over a channel you can verify and pin
# directly; the lines below are starting hints, not active entries -- fill
# the pin you verified and uncomment:
#
# <sha256>  infineon-optiga-rsa-root.pem  https://pki.infineon.com/OptigaRsaRootCA/OptigaRsaRootCA.crt  Infineon OPTIGA TPM RSA Root CA
# <sha256>  stm-tpm-ek-root.pem           https://secure.globalsign.com/cacert/stmtpmekroot.crt        STMicroelectronics TPM EK Root CA
#
# Intel (legacy upgrades.intel.com vs Intel OnDie CA for CSME PTT), AMD fTPM,
# and AMD vTPM do not reduce to a single shippable root -- walk a real EK
# certificate from the platform and pin whatever self-signed root it
# terminates at. See the README.
